hi everyone I'm ujja from Stanford  University today it is my great honor to  present you the complexity of infinite  Verizon General some stochastic games  the talk is based on joint work with  Vidya musukuma and Aaron said for  let's first look at a basic mathematical  model Markov chain  it is composed of three parts States  transition probabilities and Rewards  at every stage there is a transition  probability Vector to the other states  which characterize the transition  this could be either randomized or  deterministed  while we do not have any decisions for  players to make here we can still have  different players for a Markov chain in  the model we can define instant rewards  that each player will receive at each  state where we which we denote by r  this may be completely independent among  players in general this can be defined  for arbitrary many players too  the utility of a player is defined by  collecting all the instant rewards she  receives throughout the process there  are two well-known Notions of utilities  the average reward and discounted reward  they are well defined as long as we fix  some initial distribution over the  states  given an initial distribution the notion  of average reward computes the long-term  average of instant rewards that the  player receives throughout the process  as an example we show here how to  compute the average reward for the first  player  on the other hand the discount reward  computes the infinite sum of discounted  rewards where we discount more for steps  further in the future  here we give an example to compute the  discounted reward utility for the first  player  now we have gone over the basics of  Markov chain how is that related to our  topic today  actually the most General game model I'm  going to talk about is simultaneous  stochastic games  it is basically a generalization of  Markov chain for multiplayers by  incorporating the decision-making  process into it  besides the three basic components for  Markov chain it also has actions defined  for players  consider again our toy example with two  players  we now give these two players control on  the two states  this means that they respectively have  some action they can choose to take from  at each state and their joint actions  decide the transition probability vector  and also their instant Rewards  we got the strategy as the collection of  all players actions for each state  note that we not only allow  deterministic or pure strategies but  also allow randomized or mixed strategy  for each player  for a fixed strategy the model still  reduces to a Markov chain and the  utility of players could be measured  similarly as we mentioned  the goal for studying simultaneous  stochastic games is to find strategy  that achieves Nash equilibrium of  utilities  or do so in an approximate sense  these types of games are very prominent  in our daily life for instance in  autonomous driving the future State  depends on both other player other  drivers and your own action  and different drivers may have  completely different goals first this  becomes a natural instance of  simultaneous stochastic games  while the games we introduced previously  are very general and very natural there  are also two important special cases  which are interesting on their own  one class is known as term-based  stochastic games  basically it has the same components the  main difference is now for each state we  assign a controller and only the  controller can pick different actions at  that State  here in our toy example  green player controls the Green State  and gray player controls the gray state  only the controller's action will decide  the transition probability and the resp  reward that each player receives at the  state  here the strategy corresponds to the  collection of controllers action for  each single state which of course can  still be randomized in a mixed strategy  when fixing a strategy this still  reduces to a Markov chain and the  utility can be measured in a similar way  the goal for studying term-based  stochastic games is also to find a  strategy that achieves the Nash  equilibrium or do so in an approximate  sense  these types of games are very popular  for instance in a chess game or board  game the game iterates round by round  among the players and at each round only  one player takes the action  those are natural examples of term-based  stochastic games  putting all these classes together we  start from Markov chain which  characterize transitions on the states  when we want to further incorporate  decision making of more agent in the  games here comes turn-based stochastic  games and simultaneous stochastic games  we make a few assumptions on the model  we study  first we assume State and action spaces  are finite  we focus on learning stationary policy  which does not depend on the timestamp T  third we assume all instant rewards  received are non-negative for all  players  finally we focus on well-conditioned  class of games meaning that the model  parameter one overwhelming schema for  the discounted games and team mix the  mixing time for the average reward games  are both polynomially bounded by the  problem representation size for example  the state size and the action space size  there are a few facts that people  already know about these problems  for simultaneous games people do believe  that it is a hard problem more  concretely they are known to be at least  p-pad hard since the simultaneous  stochastic games with a single state  were reduced to a normal form games  which is known to be pee pad hard  here I'm not going to talk about the  formal definition of ppap complexity  class but basically you can think of it  as equivalent to solving an approximate  natural equilibrium of the two player  General sum games  for turn-based stochastic games people  don't have any idea about how hard this  problem is  there indeed are some facts hinting that  the problem may be easy  for instance we do know that zero sums  are turn-based games is easy  single State turn-based Games as opposed  to the simultaneous counterparts  degenerate to a single state markup  decision process and consequently are  also easy  and Computing non-stationary natural  equilibrium for turn-based stochastic  games are also known to be easy  So based on these known facts our work  give a more complete picture of the  complexity of this class of games  in particular we showed that SSG is not  harder by two player General some normal  form games by formally establishing the  membership of finding an approximate  Dash equilibrium of an ssgs inside the  p-pad complexity class  for turn-based stochastic games we show  they are just as hard as SSG although  being more structured  we proved the p-pad hardness of finding  an approximate Niche for turn-based  stochastic games  we also managed to identify an easy  subclass of turn-based games which have  a nice local reward structure  we proved for such special class of  turn-based games with localized rewards  we can indeed design polynomial time  algorithms using the potential game idea  first of all before diving into our  techniques and how we achieved all these  results I'd like to give a little detour  on the common challenge for studying  these classes of games  the common challenges the utility  function for player I which we denote by  UI as a function of strategy of player I  Pi I and other player strategy Pi minus  I this utility function is not  necessarily linear convex or concave in  the variables  to illustrate this point here is the  visualization of the utility function  for some fixed single player mdp with  five states  when fixing actions on all the other  states and only changing strategy on  state 1 linearly the blue figure shows  how the utility will change and it turns  out to be a non-linear and non-concave  function  when fixing actions on other states and  changing strategy on state 2 linearly  the red figure is non-linear and  non-convex  this suggests that applying the  classical optimization toolkit to these  problems might be tricky and try to  construct hard instance by playing with  the utility function also seems to be  tricky  so to overcome these challenges our core  idea in the study is try to linearize  all these games  the key observation we make is that  games with linear utilities are  relatively much better understood  for instance for two player General some  standard games which we use to define  the p-pad complexity class  they are a classical types of games we  mentioned earlier  that by definition has linear utilities  for each player I their utility is  characterized by a matrix AI when they  each play strategy Pi 1 and Pi 2 the  utility is simply Pi 1 transpose AI pi  2.  actually due to this nice linear  property they are the first ones that  people are able to establish ppad  complexity results for  for turn-based games and stochastic  simultaneous games we try to view them  as a generalization of General some  normal phone games  in fact although the utility functions  they have are non-linear we show they  satisfy a pseudo-linear property  to formally explain what this property  is  let's fix a player index I and the state  s fix all the other player strategy  Pi minus is and consider two different  strategies player I play at the  corresponding State s which we call Pi  is and Pi Prime is  we could Define a strategy function Pi  Theta which linearly interpolates the  strategy induced by pi and the strategy  induced by pi Prime  note when Theta changes from 0 to 1 only  the strategy of player I as state as  will change and all the other policies  will remain unchanged  the pseudo-linear property essentially  says that the slope of the utility  function of player I between any point  Pi Theta and Pi 0 is close to the slope  defined by pi 1 and Pi zero by a  multiplicative factor of row  as an illustration suppose we have the  utility at Pi 0 and Pi 1 as in the  figure  we could connect them by linear function  and we show u i Pi Theta will not have  slope two different compared with the  slope of this linear function  for any Theta between 0 and 1.  this to the linear property actually has  several nice implications  first when the multiplicative Factor rho  equals to 1 this property basically  implies the utility as a linear function  in player I strategy Pi I  so in general it could be viewed as a  generalization of the linearity  second this is actually a stronger  condition than the element-wise  monotonicity  this condition implies according to wise  monotonicity of the utility function  which actually suffices to ensure the  existence of mixed equilibrium for those  kinds of games  so this property also gives that  alternative way to prove the existence  of Nash equilibrium  also under mild extra conditions this  pseudo-linearity suffices to show that  the problem is not harder than General  some normal form games which lie in the  p-pad complexity class  finally together with other structure of  turn-based games this helps us to show  that solving turn-based games are also  as hard as general some normal form  games  this helps us to establish the p-pad  hardness result  however this hardness result is not  immediate consequence of just the  pseudo-linear utilities  to prove that we also need to design  game gadgets to implement arithmetic  circuit Gates  due to the time constraints I'm not  going into the detail of these  constructions which may be a little bit  complicated basically here are how we  Implement all the different arithmetic  Gates by carefully designing instances  of turn-based games and choosing the  right instant reward and transition  probability vectors  I'm very happy to talk more about them  during the in-person meeting  on a high level the pseudo-linear  property gives us a way to view  turn-based games and simultaneous games  has not been too different from General  some normal form games  and consequently they establish their  complexity similarly as people do for  General some normal form games  it is worth mentioning that a concurrent  independent work by Costas Noah and Kai  Ching further established the hardness  result for two-player turn-based games  another interesting part in our paper is  a positive result on efficiently solving  structured turn-based games with  localized Rewards  the way we achieve this result is by  viewing these types of games as variant  of potential games  in Game Theory people have defined  potential games or Markov potential  games where there exists a potential  function Phi such that the difference of  the potential function's value could  basically capture all the differences in  each player's utility assuming the  player that player has changed her  strategy  on the high level we try to relate  turn-based games with this extra  localized reward structure to Market  potential games  the special case of turn-based games we  study are local reward instances  basically we assume each player could  only control at most one state and only  receive a non-negative instant reward at  the state that she controls  for all the other states she doesn't  have control  she will only receive a zero instant  reward  we caught this kind of instance as local  reward turn-based games  under such assumptions of localized  reward by algebra we can write the  utility function as this nice form  for each player I the utility is a  product of a function fi that only  depends on player I strategy times of  function GI that only depends on other  players strategy divided by a general  function h  age depends on all player strategy but  are the same for all players and doesn't  depend on I  under such structural property we can  show the utility ratio between the two  policies can be essentially bounded by  the ratio of some defined potential  function Phi for the five properly  defined here  because of this nice ratio equivalence  property this essentially established  the potential condition we need for  Markov potential games in the  logarithmic space  this log space potential game condition  allow us to show several nice properties  for such games  first we can show there must exist pure  strategy Nash where no player needs to  play a mixed strategy  second this allows us to design  polynomial time algorithm for the  problem  the polynomial time algorithm we  consider is a type of method that  basically just alternate between the  coordinates and prove it converges in  polynomial time  by showing at every sub-optimal point we  are able to make big enough progress in  terms of the potential function  this helps us to show that the  alternating coordinate wise algorithm  indeed is a polynomial time algorithm  and this class of localized reward  turn-based games indeed is a class of  games that can be efficiently solved  so putting all the things together and  in conclusion we show the complexity  picture for multiplayer games about  finding an approximate Nash equilibrium  in either the simultaneous stochastic  games or the turn-based stochastic games  we show Finding approximate Nash in both  these kinds of General games are ppac  complete  we also design new efficient polynomial  time algorithms for solving structured  turn-based games  these kinds of results all come from  relating these games to other classes of  well-studied games  for instance for simultaneous and  turn-based stochastic games in general  we reduce them to show the equivalence  with two-player General some normal form  games which has a linear utility  structure  for turn-based stochastic games with  localized reward we relate them to the  mark of potential games and show that  they satisfy this potential game  condition in the logarithmic space  we hope these ideas of relating  different classes of games could find  further utility for studying broader  classes of multiplayer games too  this is the end of my talk thank you  very much  I'd be happy to take any questions and  discuss more about the details of our  work during the in-person conference  thank you
 

